{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.GaussianThetaSum","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.GaussianThetaSum","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.NumberTheory.ModularForms.JacobiTheta.TwoVariable"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.hasSum_gaussian_theta_real","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.hasSum_gaussian_theta_real","kind":"theorem","line":216,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/GaussianThetaSum.lean#L216-L238","doc":"The real form: `∑_{n∈ℤ} e^{−a(n−c)²} = √(π/a)·Re θ₂(−c, iπ/a)`, as a\n`HasSum` over `ℤ` of nonnegative reals.\n\n(The theta value is in fact real — pairing `n ↔ −n` — but taking the real part\nis enough for the Laplace application and avoids that extra argument.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.tsum_gaussian_theta","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.tsum_gaussian_theta","kind":"theorem","line":210,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/GaussianThetaSum.lean#L210-L214","doc":"The `tsum` form of `hasSum_gaussian_theta`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.hasSum_gaussian_theta_tilt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.hasSum_gaussian_theta_tilt","kind":"theorem","line":175,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/GaussianThetaSum.lean#L175-L208","doc":"**The tilted sum — every Gaussian moment at once.**\n\n`∑_{n∈ℤ} e^{−a(n−c)² + t(n−c)} = e^{t²/4a}·√(π/a)·θ₂(−(c + t/2a), iπ/a)`.\n\nCompleting the square is a shift of `c` by `t/2a`, so this is `hasSum_gaussian_theta`\nwith no new analytic input.  It is the moment generating function of the discrete\nGaussian: the `m`-th `t`-derivative at `0` is `∑_k (k−c)^m e^{−a(k−c)²}`, and the\n`e^{t²/4a}·√(π/a)` prefactor is exactly the CONTINUUM generating function\n`∫_ℝ e^{−ax²+tx} dx`.  The entire discrete-vs-continuum discrepancy, at every\nmoment, is therefore the single factor `θ₂(−(c + t/2a), iπ/a) = 1 + O(e^{−π²/a})`,\nuniformly in `c` and `t`.\n\nThat is the sense in which the cubic-and-higher remainder of a Laplace potential\ncannot produce a polynomial correction: it enters only through moments, and the\nmoments are exponentially faithful.  (The differentiation step itself is not\nformalised here — see the file header.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.hasSum_gaussian_theta","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.hasSum_gaussian_theta","kind":"theorem","line":150,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/GaussianThetaSum.lean#L150-L173","doc":"**`∑_{n∈ℤ} e^{−a(n−c)²} = √(π/a) · θ₂(−c, iπ/a)`**, as a `HasSum`, for every\n`a > 0` and every real shift `c`.\n\nExact — no asymptotics.  The `n = 0` term of `θ₂(−c, iπ/a)` is `1` and the rest\nis `O(e^{−π²/a})`, while `√(π/a) = ∫_ℝ e^{−a(x−c)²} dx`; so this is the precise\nstatement that replacing the Laplace sum by the Laplace integral costs only an\nexponentially small relative error, uniformly in the shift `c`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.jacobiTheta₂_gaussian_functional_equation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.jacobiTheta₂_gaussian_functional_equation","kind":"theorem","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/GaussianThetaSum.lean#L122-L148","doc":"`jacobiTheta₂_functional_equation` at `τ = ia/π`, `z = −iac/π`, with the\nprefactor `1/(−iτ)^{1/2}` identified as the real `(π/a)^{1/2}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.hasSum_gaussian_theta_aux","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.hasSum_gaussian_theta_aux","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/GaussianThetaSum.lean#L112-L120","doc":"The shifted Gaussian family is summable over `ℤ`, with sum the theta value —\nthis is `hasSum_jacobiTheta₂_term` transported along\n`jacobiTheta₂_term_gaussian`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.im_I_mul_div_pi_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.im_I_mul_div_pi_pos","kind":"theorem","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/GaussianThetaSum.lean#L105-L110","doc":"`ia/π` lies in the upper half plane for `a > 0`, which is what\n`jacobiTheta₂` needs for convergence. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.jacobiTheta₂_term_gaussian","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.jacobiTheta₂_term_gaussian","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/GaussianThetaSum.lean#L94-L103","doc":"The `jacobiTheta₂` term at `τ = ia/π`, `z = −iac/π` is a shifted Gaussian:\n`exp(2πinz + πin²τ) = e^{ac²}·e^{−a(n−c)²}`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}